                "authorType": "COMMENT",
                "competitionRanking": 15,
                "content": "<blockquote>\n  <p>When I trained, I used a Blackwell GPU, but when I tried to commit and submit, I selected \"Run with GPU for all/this sessions\" and Kaggle used a P100 instead. I also have submission.zip in my working directory in Kaggle but it's 3GB and I can't download it through the browser. Is there another way to submit this file?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>With persistence enabled (files only) you could start a new CPU-only session (without any accelerator and with Internet enabled) and submit to the competition by running a command like:</p>\n<p><code>!kaggle competitions submit -c nvidia-nemotron-model-reasoning-challenge -f submission.zip -m \"My first submission\"</code></p>\n<p>In such a way you would avoid consuming some of your GPU quota in the submission process. </p>",
                "feedbackTrackingData": {},
                "id": 3435755,
                "isCollapsed": true,
                "isThankYou": false,
                "kernelVersion": 0,
                "parent": {},
                "postDate": "2026-04-04T17:20:59.157Z",
                "rawMarkdown": "> When I trained, I used a Blackwell GPU, but when I tried to commit and submit, I selected \"Run with GPU for all/this sessions\" and Kaggle used a P100 instead. I also have submission.zip in my working directory in Kaggle but it's 3GB and I can't download it through the browser. Is there another way to submit this file?\n\nWith persistence enabled (files only) you could start a new CPU-only session (without any accelerator and with Internet enabled) and submit to the competition by running a command like:\n\n`!kaggle competitions submit -c nvidia-nemotron-model-reasoning-challenge -f submission.zip -m \"My first submission\"`\n\nIn such a way you would avoid consuming some of your GPU quota in the submission process. \n\n",
